	 ('558f362d-4e68-4151-9bc0-93f6c1cbc3a8'::uuid,'WHIRLING ARM','DEVICE','{"{\"название\": \"WHIRLING ARM\", \"описание\": \"The WHIRLING ARM is a device originally designed for measuring wind resistance (drag) on rotating sp\"}"}','The WHIRLING ARM is a device originally designed for measuring wind resistance (drag) on rotating spheres or plates, first utilized by Robins in 1742. This apparatus has since been refined and expanded upon by subsequent inventors such as John Smeaton and Hume Rouse to accommodate a broader range of aerodynamic tests. Over time, the WHIRLING ARM''s applications have broadened significantly, making it instrumental not only for measuring drag but also for testing windmill, aerofoil, and wing resistance. By incorporating specific radius rotations, this device allows researchers to observe air flow patterns and forces on various objects in circular motion.

The importance of the WHIRLING ARM extends beyond its practical functions; it has played a crucial role in early aviation design. It provided critical data for understanding wing forces and lift coefficients before engineers could develop larger-scale flight experiments, such as the steam monoplane. As a fundamental tool in aerodynamic r','{}','{}','{"frequency": 4, "text_unit_ids": ["4089000a7a5f43c1f79ebd955ed69dab8fc13c6f0153fe4c6ae9df3fdfa0ecdfc28e259193c210fc04e64e6f124fe76ccb1f7ccbb3e5ccb8e8d848fbdfd2ef5c", "55e473f9ecac8cd67790067e6eab0198e01f4defc514be87f835b5c567883e65d857bb0c55de712e20d7bed24782b9a7ad7f49d93a4ae738a71114a86c61a98d", "55e473f9ecac8cd67790067e6eab0198e01f4defc514be87f835b5c567883e65d857bb0c55de712e20d7bed24782b9a7ad7f49d93a4ae738a71114a86c61a98d", "16d966814983125a6e32b061e1707e94f1e8d9d91c7fb8fece809b8d03586b149afc62f017609bda9a3a36cbfcdfd60df5527110c12c82e5fcd08b8eb20847b0"]}','2026-07-17 13:19:24.183818','2026-07-17 13:19:24.183818','history_development_aeronautics'),
	 ('75c36b22-81b1-4081-a9a0-592039aecc52'::uuid,'OTTO LILIENTHAL','PERSON','{"{\"название\": \"OTTO LILIENTHAL\", \"описание\": \"Otto Lilienthal, a German engineer and aviation pioneer, made significant contributions to early avi\"}"}','Otto Lilienthal, a German engineer and aviation pioneer, made significant contributions to early aviation through his systematic experimentation and glider design. Born in 1848, Lilienthal is best known for his extensive work in hang gliding, conducting over 2,500 successful flights between 1891 and 1896 before his tragic death in a crash on August 10, 1896.

Lilienthal’s pioneering experiments began in 1874 when he conducted free-wind tests that validated theoretical models about aerofoil drag, lift, and polar characteristics. His research confirmed that cambered shapes provided better performance compared to flat plates, validating the importance of airfoil design for aircraft. Building on this work and the experiments conducted by Wenham in earlier years, Lilienthal published his findings in 1889 and went on to develop a series of highly innovative gliders.

