	 ('43cddebb-d5d8-46c4-ac3e-d502f8ad3f96'::uuid,'QUARTZ','MODEL','{"{\"название\": \"QUARTZ\", \"описание\": \"Quartz is a crystalline form of silicon dioxide (SiO₂) characterized by its specific and well-define\"}"}','Quartz is a crystalline form of silicon dioxide (SiO₂) characterized by its specific and well-defined lattice parameters. This structured arrangement of atoms gives quartz unique physical properties, making it applicable in various contexts beyond its basic chemical composition.','{}','{}','{"frequency": 2, "text_unit_ids": ["53b04af596a3f009b0b172d98500b34c3db439936f372536e19461beaac3c19f398b3faf0edd2566c35cf6cf60e602f2e382880f31675f6dbd7eb9926bc2496f", "53b04af596a3f009b0b172d98500b34c3db439936f372536e19461beaac3c19f398b3faf0edd2566c35cf6cf60e602f2e382880f31675f6dbd7eb9926bc2496f"]}','2026-07-17 17:14:05.801343','2026-07-17 17:14:05.801343','solid_state_physics_v1'),
	 ('b4077134-be48-47d7-8981-8df8804d8264'::uuid,'CR','MATERIAL','{"{\"название\": \"CR\", \"описание\": \"Chromium, with an atomic radius of 0.127 nm and a lattice parameter of 0.288 nm\"}"}','Chromium, with an atomic radius of 0.127 nm and a lattice parameter of 0.288 nm','{}','{}','{"frequency": 1, "text_unit_ids": ["53b04af596a3f009b0b172d98500b34c3db439936f372536e19461beaac3c19f398b3faf0edd2566c35cf6cf60e602f2e382880f31675f6dbd7eb9926bc2496f"]}','2026-07-17 17:14:05.801343','2026-07-17 17:14:05.801343','solid_state_physics_v1'),
	 ('bc626108-19e4-4d77-bfcb-29e430aa4023'::uuid,'ALTITUDE','MEASUREMENT','{}','Parameter for the height above a reference level within the atmosphere','{}','{}','{"frequency": 1, "text_unit_ids": ["22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5"]}','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','aerodynamics_v1'),
	 ('0581b1e0-fea0-4bda-b2a8-5185b351c4b6'::uuid,'CONTINUUM MODEL','MODEL','{"{\"название\": \"CONTINUUM MODEL\", \"описание\": \"The CONTINUUM MODEL is a theoretical framework employed for examining phenomena occurring at altitud\"}"}','The CONTINUUM MODEL is a theoretical framework employed for examining phenomena occurring at altitudes where the mean free path of gas molecules becomes significantly smaller compared to the dimensions of objects in the atmosphere. This model finds particular application in aerodynamics, specifically when studying conditions up to an altitude of approximately 80 kilometers. At these heights, the mean free path is notably small relative to characteristic object dimensions, making it necessary to use this continuum approach rather than methods applicable to rarefied gases or space phenomena where such assumptions do not hold. The CONTINUUM MODEL thus provides a robust analytical tool for understanding atmospheric behaviors and fluid dynamics in the upper layers of Earth''s atmosphere.','{}','{}','{"frequency": 2, "text_unit_ids": ["22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5", "22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5"]}','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','aerodynamics_v1'),
	 ('6ec74472-ab7e-420d-aae7-2e6fe516427f'::uuid,'MACH NUMBER M','MECHANISM','{"{\"название\": \"MACH NUMBER M\", \"описание\": \"Mach Number M is a crucial dimensionless ratio that quantifies the relationship between an aircraft’\"}"}','Mach Number M is a crucial dimensionless ratio that quantifies the relationship between an aircraft’s true flight speed and the local speed of sound. This metric is essential in assessing the approach to sonic conditions and their impact on aircraft performance issues, including aerodynamic changes and structural stresses. The Mach number serves as a key parameter for classifying fluid flows around supersonic, transonic, and subsonic speeds. By using this value, engineers can select appropriate aerodynamic models and designs that ensure optimal performance under various flight regimes, thereby addressing potential challenges associated with accelerating towards and beyond the speed of sound.','{}','{}','{"frequency": 3, "text_unit_ids": ["22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5", "b1ce24296a58526324fabc7ae6e490936e4736e1162bc2970189c9eac707fa913c796b5d317389c1344080f396eba1801f7b804ce60b31231b538920f900bf69", "b1ce24296a58526324fabc7ae6e490936e4736e1162bc2970189c9eac707fa913c796b5d317389c1344080f396eba1801f7b804ce60b31231b538920f900bf69"]}','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','aerodynamics_v1'),
	 ('01195880-4174-427a-921d-fae0791b0d40'::uuid,'IDEAL INCOMPRESSIBLE GAS MODEL','MODEL','{"{\"название\": \"IDEAL INCOMPRESSIBLE GAS MODEL\", \"описание\": \"The IDEAL INCOMPRESSIBLE GAS MODEL is a simplified theoretical framework used primarily in fluid dyn\"}"}','The IDEAL INCOMPRESSIBLE GAS MODEL is a simplified theoretical framework used primarily in fluid dynamics to analyze and predict the behavior of gases under various conditions. This model considers only the mass of the gas particles as an essential factor, disregarding other complexities such as viscosity, compressibility, and intermolecular forces. By focusing solely on the mass of the medium particles, this model significantly streamlines the analysis, making complex fluid dynamics problems more manageable for both theoretical study and practical application. This simplification enables engineers and scientists to derive accurate approximations without the need to account for less prominent factors that could complicate calculations but have minimal impact in certain scenarios.','{}','{}','{"frequency": 2, "text_unit_ids": ["22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5", "22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5"]}','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','aerodynamics_v1'),
	 ('1cbb7337-c3e1-49c8-b21a-fa89a6693b8e'::uuid,'PRESSURE','MEASUREMENT','{}','Measure of force per unit area in the atmosphere','{}','{}','{"frequency": 1, "text_unit_ids": ["22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5"]}','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','aerodynamics_v1'),
	 ('421702f7-6669-4d82-a882-3b339408aff1'::uuid,'MOLECULES','PARTICLE','{}','Particles that make up the air in the atmosphere, their behavior affects physical parameters like mean free path','{}','{}','{"frequency": 1, "text_unit_ids": ["22c3c2c423ad221ca214710cfa41f27d1e4dc8d6d2a92cf697c6e413557740e0d79914704f6f8c0e7f4ce8f21bf52fe6505883477ed45d7e212012e482c40ef5"]}','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','2026-07-17 16:18:55.489892','aerodynamics_v1');
